# Example of generic dispatch behavior:
# If we pass a non-standard list object to summary() or plot()
custom_list <- list(a = 1:5, b = "test")

# summary() works because a default method (summary.default) exists
summary(custom_list)

# However, applying a domain-specific generics like mean() directly to a full data frame: 
# mean(mtcars) Triggers a warning/error in R.
# This is because generic functions rely on method dispatch. So, if no specific method 
# (e.g., mean.data.frame) exists for a given class, R falls back to the default method,
# which may fail if the underlying data structure isn't compatible.

